Description: obs-ffmpeg: the custom output's raw callbacks and its stop share a lock on the encoder's data
 ffmpeg_output_full_stop() calls obs_output_end_data_capture(), which disconnects the output's raw video and audio
 callbacks later, on a thread of libobs's own (end_data_capture_thread), and then ffmpeg_deactivate() at once, which
 frees the codec contexts, the frames and the muxer (ffmpeg_data_free()). A frame the video thread was still handing to
 receive_video() then went to a freed encoder: measured on OBS 33.0.1 with FFmpeg's libx264 into Matroska, 2 stops in
 100 ended in SIGSEGV in av_buffer_unref() under avcodec_send_frame() (receive_video(),
 obs-ffmpeg-output.c:720, called from libobs's video thread), after x264 had printed its closing statistics.
 The two callbacks now hold a mutex while they encode, and ffmpeg_deactivate() and the start's ffmpeg_data_init() hold
 it while the data are freed or made; a callback that comes after the stop finds the data cleared and returns. The
 lock order is this mutex, then write_mutex, as before.
 Upstream reports the same race from its other side, not fixed: obs-studio issue 14153 (a frame from the previous
 recording leaks into the next one).
Author: Daniel Campos Ramos
Origin: other, Sparky Stereo OS (ORDER 61, milestone 3, continuation 6)
Bug: https://github.com/obsproject/obs-studio/issues/14153
Forwarded: no, OBS asks for human-written submissions only (CONTRIBUTING.md, AI/Machine Learning Policy)
Last-Update: 2026-11-23
